cartridge "GALACTIC CAB CO./C.BLAKEMORE/1987"

' GALACTIC CAB CO.
' A ColecoVision game based on "Galactic Cab Co." by Cleveland M.
' Blakemore, Ahoy! June 1987 (Commodore 128, BASIC 7.0 with its SPRITE,
' MOVSPR, BUMP and COLLISION commands). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you fly a hover cab in a cavern on a far moon. Seven fares wait
' on landing pads. Touch a fare to pick it up (the cab gets heavier). Any
' touch of rock, pad or door wrecks the cab and ends the game. With three
' fares or more aboard, the airlock at the top starts to open and close:
' fly out through it while it is open for a $1000 bonus per fare, then a
' new run begins in the same cavern with your cash kept.
'
' From the original (orig/galacticcab.bas.txt): the jets fire only while
' the button is held, with no direction (up), up, up-left, up-right, left
' or right; every thrust has the same upward push, and left / right add the
' same sideways push (down and its diagonals fire nothing); each thrust
' burns one unit of the 500 of fuel, with none left the jets are dead;
' gravity starts with the first thrust and grows by a quarter with every
' fare (G .06 + .015 a fare); there is no drag; the seven fares 300, 200,
' 100, 500, 600, 800 and 1000 on their pads, their fare written by each pad;
' a random passenger waves at every step; the airlock opens only with more
' than two fares, opening and closing for ever (T 0 to 6 and back in half
' steps; open from 4); flying out pays $1000 a fare, counted up with a
' ring, "SUCCESSFUL RUN !!!" and a new run with fuel, gravity and fares
' reset; a crash: the cab bursts into pieces, then "YOU HAVE MADE $... IN
' CASH". The status line: FUEL, V, H, FARES, CASH.
'
' Liberties: a new cavern drawn for the ColecoVision's 28 safe columns
' (the same plan: pads on both walls, a pillar, two floor pads, the airlock
' in the ceiling's middle) and a round hover cab; the C128 ran one step per
' pass of a slow BASIC loop: here one step is 4 frames (15 a second) and the
' cab moves smoothly every frame (thrust .2 and gravity .06 pixel per step
' become 51 and 15 4096ths of a pixel per frame per frame, one fuel unit per
' 4 frames of thrust); collisions are tile tests at six points of the cab's
' outline and a box test with the fares (the C128 used its sprite
' collision registers); V and H show pixels a second; every successful run
' makes the next one a little harder (gravity +1 up to +5, 40 less fuel
' down to 300), so one memorised route can't be flown for ever on the same
' terms; the airlock's iris is 6 tiles (C128: 7 characters) and moves
' a half step every 8 frames, so it stays open about 1.7 seconds: a full
' cab climbs slowly, and at 4 frames a step it closed before even a perfect
' climb from a hover under it could get through; the best cash
' is kept; GAME OVER then the title (the C128 asked ANOTHER GAME? (Y/N)).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): hold a side button to fire the jets; the
' stick (none, up, left, right and the upper diagonals) aims them. On the
' title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs cab-print-at-two-indexed-coords removed 2026-11-01.

